{
  "id": "6c5099ea-3bb9-4042-a805-f77db7a893cf",
  "notes": "**EXPLANATION AND DEMONSTRATION OF THE 3-SENTENCE RULE FOR STRUCTURING MESSAGES**  \n\n**Introduction to the 3-Sentence Rule**  \nThe 3-sentence rule is a concise communication technique designed to improve clarity and efficiency in professional or academic messages. By structuring your thoughts into three purposeful sentences, you ensure your message is direct, scannable, and actionable.  \n\n- **Purpose**: Minimize ambiguity and save time for both sender and receiver.  \n- **Ideal Use Cases**: Emails, Slack messages, meeting requests, or any scenario requiring brevity.  \n- **Key Benefit**: Forces prioritization of essential information.  \n\n**Structure of the 3-Sentence Rule**  \nEach sentence serves a distinct function to create a complete yet compact message:  \n\n1. **Sentence 1: Context or Purpose**  \n   - Open with a clear statement of why you’re communicating.  \n   - Example: *\"I’m following up on our project deadline discussion from yesterday.\"*  \n\n2. **Sentence 2: Detail or Request**  \n   - Provide necessary specifics or ask a direct question.  \n   - Example: *\"Could you confirm if the timeline aligns with the client’s expectations?\"*  \n\n3. **Sentence 3: Action or Next Steps**  \n   - Clarify what happens next (e.g., deadline, response needed).  \n   - Example: *\"Please reply by EOD Friday so I can update the team.\"*  \n\n**Demonstration of the Rule in Practice**  \nCompare these two versions of the same message:  \n\n- **Without the Rule**: A lengthy paragraph mixing questions, details, and unclear expectations.  \n- **With the Rule**:  \n  - *\"I’m sharing the revised budget for your review.\"* (Purpose)  \n  - *\"Slide 3 highlights the 10% reduction in marketing costs.\"* (Detail)  \n  - *\"Let me know if you approve by Tuesday, or we can discuss in our sync.\"* (Action)  \n\n**Tips for Effective Implementation**  \n- **Audience Awareness**: Adjust tone/formality (e.g., peer vs. executive).  \n- **Exceptions**: Use more sentences only if absolutely necessary (e.g., legal disclaimers).  \n- **Practice**: Draft messages, then trim to three sentences.  \n\n**References**  \n- *Harvard Business Review*: \"The Art of the Concise Email\" (2021)  \n- *The 3-Sentence Rule* (G. Reynolds, *Communication Essentials*, 2019)  \n\n**Exercise**  \nRewrite one of your recent messages using the 3-sentence rule. Note how it changes readability and response time."
}
