  "notes": "**HANDS-ON ACTIVITY: IDENTIFYING AND ELIMINATING FILLER WORDS IN SAMPLE SPEECHES**  \n\n**Introduction to Filler Words**  \nFiller words are unnecessary words or sounds (e.g., \"um,\" \"like,\" \"you know\") that disrupt the flow of speech and reduce clarity. This activity helps you recognize and remove them to improve communication effectiveness.  \n\n- **Why it matters**:  \n  - Filler words weaken your message and reduce credibility.  \n  - Eliminating them makes speeches more concise and engaging.  \n  - Professional settings (e.g., presentations, interviews) demand polished delivery.  \n\n**Steps to Identify Filler Words**  \n1. **Listen Actively**: Play the sample speech and note every filler word.  \n2. **Transcribe & Highlight**: Write the speech verbatim and mark fillers.  \n3. **Categorize**: Group fillers by type (e.g., pauses, repetitions).  \n\n**Strategies for Elimination**  \n- **Pause Instead**: Replace fillers with brief silences for emphasis.  \n- **Practice Slowly**: Record yourself and gradually increase speed.  \n- **Use Scripts**: Prepare key phrases to avoid improvisation traps.  \n\n**Activity Instructions**  \n1. **Analyze the Sample**: Use the steps above to identify 5+ filler words.  \n2. **Rewrite**: Remove fillers while preserving the original meaning.  \n3. **Compare**: Contrast the original and revised versions for impact.  \n\n**Example Approach**  \n- Original: \"So, um, the project was, like, really challenging...\"  \n- Revised: \"The project was challenging...\"  \n\n**References**  \n- *Talk Like TED* by Carmine Gallo (authoritative guide on persuasive speaking).  \n- Toastmasters International (practical training for public speaking).  \n\n**Reflection Questions**  \n- How did removing fillers change the speech’s tone?  \n- Which filler words do you commonly use? How will you address them?  \n\n**Pro Tip**: Practice with a peer for feedback—external perspectives reveal blind spots."
