  "id": "ebd1efb6-6405-4c5f-9c5c-dde89d204463",
  "notes": "**GROUP ACTIVITY: PEER REVIEW OF RECORDED ELEVATOR PITCHES FOR CLARITY AND CONCISENESS**  \n\n**Introduction to Peer Review in Elevator Pitches**  \nPeer review is a collaborative learning technique where you evaluate each other's work to provide constructive feedback. In this activity, you will assess recorded elevator pitches for clarity (how well the message is understood) and conciseness (how efficiently the message is delivered).  \n\n- **Purpose**: Improve communication skills by identifying strengths and areas for improvement in pitch delivery.  \n- **Benefits**: Gain diverse perspectives, refine your own pitch, and develop critical evaluation skills.  \n- **Key Focus Areas**:  \n  - Message clarity (Is the core idea easy to grasp?)  \n  - Conciseness (Is the pitch brief yet impactful?)  \n  - Delivery (Is the tone, pace, and body language effective?)  \n\n**Steps for Effective Peer Review**  \nFollow this structured approach to ensure meaningful feedback:  \n\n1. **Watch the Pitch**:  \n   - Take notes on first impressions.  \n   - Identify the main idea within the first 10 seconds.  \n\n2. **Evaluate Clarity**:  \n   - Is the purpose of the pitch immediately clear?  \n   - Are technical terms explained (if applicable)?  \n   - Does the structure (problem-solution-benefit) flow logically?  \n\n3. **Assess Conciseness**:  \n   - Is the pitch under 60 seconds?  \n   - Are there redundant phrases or filler words (e.g., \"um,\" \"like\")?  \n   - Could the same message be delivered with fewer words?  \n\n4. **Provide Constructive Feedback**:  \n   - Use the \"Sandwich Method\": Start with a positive, suggest improvements, end encouragingly.  \n   - Example: *\"Your energy is engaging! To make the value proposition clearer, try stating the problem upfront. Great job overall!\"*  \n\n**Common Pitfalls to Avoid**  \n- **Vague Feedback**: \"It was good\" → Instead, specify *why* (e.g., \"Your confident tone made the pitch persuasive\").  \n- **Overemphasis on Delivery**: Avoid focusing only on speech pace; balance with content critique.  \n- **Bias**: Base feedback on objective criteria, not personal preferences.  \n\n**Activity Reflection**  \nAfter reviews, reflect on:  \n- Patterns in feedback received (e.g., multiple peers noted unclear phrasing).  \n- How to apply insights to revise your own pitch.  \n\n**References**  \n- *Harvard Business Review*: \"The Art of the Elevator Pitch\" (guidelines on brevity and impact).  \n- Toastmasters International: Techniques for clear public speaking.  \n\n**Pro Tip**: Record your revised pitch and compare it to the original to measure progress!"
